  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 49}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-7}{x-49}=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 49}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-7}{x-49} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{49}-7}{49-49}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 49}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-7}{x-49} & ={lim}_{x\\to 49}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-7)(\\sqrt{x}+7)}{(x-49)(\\sqrt{x}+7)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 49}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x})^{2}-{7}^{2}}{(x-49)(\\sqrt{x}+7)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 49}\\frac{x-49}{(x-49)(\\sqrt{x}+7)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 49}\\frac{\\cancel{x-49}}{\\cancel{(x-49)}(\\sqrt{x}+7)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 49}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+7} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{49}+7} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7+7} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
